WEBVTT

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:05.140
LLM API endpoints should require authentication because the service consumes resources and

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.865
may process sensitive data.

00:00:06.865 --> 00:00:09.350
API keys, service tokens, workload identity,

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:14.130
or user-bound credentials can identify which caller is making a request.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.080
Authentication alone is not enough.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.015
A valid caller may still need limits on models, tenants, data sources, tools,

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:22.175
request size, rate, or allowed operations.

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:24.015
Authorization should be part of the application design,

00:00:24.015 --> 00:00:25.515
not an afterthought in the model prompt.

00:00:25.515 --> 00:00:29.240
A user should only be able to submit prompts against data and functions they are allowed

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.200
to access.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.990
Tenant separation matters when the same service supports multiple customers, teams,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:34.720
or workspaces.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.290
Least privilege also applies to applications that call the LLM service.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.435
A small internal tool should not automatically receive broad access to every model and

00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:43.100
dataset.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.280
Exposure control is especially important for private inference endpoints.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.675
Local-only binding, LAN access, reverse proxies, TLS, network segmentation,

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:53.675
and firewall rules all influence who can reach the service.

00:00:53.675 --> 00:00:57.995
A model endpoint that seems harmless on localhost can become a risk if it is accidentally

00:00:57.995 --> 00:01:01.800
bound to all interfaces or exposed through a misconfigured proxy.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.885
Avoid unnecessary internet exposure for experimental or private services.

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:08.750
Operational teams should know where credentials live, how they are rotated,

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.190
how callers are revoked, and how usage is audited.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.620
Default keys, shared tokens,

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.907
and undocumented service accounts create accountability problems.

00:01:15.907 --> 00:01:20.540
Strong API boundaries help prevent unauthorized resource use, accidental data exposure,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.250
and uncontrolled access to models that may be expensive or sensitive to operate.
